  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SPVO &#x2013; Supporting smallholder farmers&apos; livelihoods in the specialty coffee value chain</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project, led by This Side Up Coffee (TSU) and Abakundakawa Rushashi Cooperative, aims to address inequality and poverty in the coffee value chain in Northern Rwanda. While Abakundakawa produces specialty coffee of high quality, the limited production and small landholdings hinder the ability to lift coffee farmers out of poverty, which often results in inability of families to support school fees and is the main driver behind the prevalence of child labour.Moreover, the inaccessibility of healthcare, as well as developing local infrastructure that can support women and young mothers care for their children is very weak or almost non-existent.This project aims to address these issues by supporting female farmers by creating a childcare and basic healthcare facility available to all members of the cooperative, as well as other interventions aimed at improving incomes from coffee and other income generating activities.During Phase A in early 2025, the consortium consisting of TSU, Abakundakawa cooperative and World Vision will conduct a baseline study with the aim to identify other suitable interventions. Ideas, such as the scale-up of the regenerative agriculture programme, activities aimed at improving availability, quality and accessibility of education, awareness raising, and other interventions will be explored.</narrative>
  </description>
